# 10.9's CFNetwork folds repeated Set-Cookie headers into one comma-joined field inside its HTTP message
# parser -- CFHTTPMessageCopyAllHeaderFields and CFHTTPMessageCopySerializedMessage both give back one
# line for two headers, so the boundary is gone before any cookie code runs. A comma inside a single
# Set-Cookie is then indistinguishable from that fold: "test=18; foo=bar, a=b" is byte-identical to the
# fold of "test=18; foo=bar" and "a=b". These two tests want the first reading; a real response's two
# headers want the second, so the division stays the one 10.9's own parser makes.
